. }  (13. c5!  dxc5 14. d5! {the point is that if black ignores it, white can force a horrendous queenside structures with dxc6. If black takes then white's knight jumps in and threatens nc7. } cxd5  (14... f4  15. dxc6 bxc6 16. Bd3  (16. exf4  Nxf4)) 15. Nxd5 {[%cal Gd5c7]}) Nc5 {[%clk 0:13:36.8] } 14. Qc2 {[%clk 0:11:15.9] } a5 {[%clk 0:13:43.7] } 15. Nd4 {[%clk 0:11:13.6] } g5 {[%clk 0:13:07.6] } 16. dxc6 {[%clk 0:10:50] } bxc6 {[%clk 0:13:15.2] } 17. Nxc6 {[%clk 0:10:23.9] } Qxc6 {[%clk 0:12:41.1] } 18. Bxh5 {[%clk 0:10:18] } g4 {[%clk 0:12:45.1] } 19. Nd5 {[%clk 0:10:01.4] } Bb7 {[%clk 0:12:32.1] } 20. f3! {[%clk 0:09:54.9] great move by me, immediately challenging black's wedge.} Qd7 {[%clk 0:09:54.9] } 21. Nb6 {[%clk 0:08:16.8] } Qe6 {[%clk 0:09:51.6] } 22. Bf4? {[%clk 0:07:23.1] here I was seeing ghosts. I correctly calculated that I could geet away with nxa1 because I have qf7 but I got scareed and played bf4 to coveer d3. Perhaps because of the psychological advantage my opponent had as a titled player. } gxf3 {[%clk 0:09:11.2] } 23. Nxa8 {[%clk 0:06:53.2] } Bxa8 {[%clk 0:05:15.8] } 24. gxf3? {[%clk 0:06:07.3] My first instinct was the betteer bxc3 not letting the bishop go unchallenged, but i talked myself out of it and thought stopping the knight getting to e4 was better (even though It didn't come with a concrete threat. } Qf6 {[%clk 0:03:15] } 25. Qf2 {[%clk 0:05:49.3] } Rg8 {[%clk 0:03:00.3] } 26. Kh1 {[%clk 0:05:34.2] } e5 {[%clk 0:03:03.4] } 27. Bh2 {[%clk 0:05:35.9] } Rb8 {[%clk 0:01:55.5] } 28. Rab1 {[%clk 0:05:18.7] } Rb6 {[%clk 0:01:58] } 29. Rg1 {[%clk 0:05:07.1] } Nd3 {[%clk 0:01:35.6] } 30. Qg3? {[%clk 0:05:04.8] This was played relatively quickly. I should have seen that f4 noww comes with tempo. I still had 5 minutes on the clock and the position was critical. I should have spent a lot longer on this move. }  (30. Qg2! {Now f4 doesn't come with tempo and exf4 isn't forced. white can simply close the centre if f4 is played here}  f4 31. e4) f4 {[%clk 0:01:29.8] } 31. exf4 {[%clk 0:04:28.6] } Nxf4 {[%clk 0:01:38.3] } 32. Bg4? {[%clk 0:03:55.2] }  (32. Qxg7+ {I did actually consider this and it wwas the best move! but perhaps impatience got to me, I incorrectly evaluated the end position. }  Qxg7 33. Rxg7 Kxg7 34. Bxf4! {I missed this at the end of the line, no f3 can stay defended and white consolidates up a pawn. }) h5 {[%clk 0:01:40] } 33. Bf5 {[%clk 0:03:40.9] } Ne2 {[%clk 0:01:19.6] } 34. Qg2 {[%clk 0:03:45.2] } Nxg1 {[%clk 0:00:55.7] } 35. Rxg1 {[%clk 0:03:35.9] } Rxb2 {[%clk 0:00:58.3] } 36. Qxb2 {[%clk 0:03:26.2] } Bxf3+ {[%clk 0:01:06.7] } 37. Rg2 {[%clk 0:03:35.8] } Qxf5 {[%clk 0:01:15.6] } 38. Kg1 {[%clk 0:03:31.4] } Bxg2 {[%clk 0:01:22] } 39. Qxg2 {[%clk 0:03:35.5] } Qb1+ {[%clk 0:01:27.7] } 40. Qf1 {[%clk 0:03:44.3] } Qxf1+ {[%clk 0:01:29.7] } 41. Kxf1 {[%clk 0:03:53.2] so why is this endgame not a theoretical draw? It's because of my h pawn which is fixed on a dark square and with same coloured bishops. This is an eternal liability, combined with the extra passed pawn black has, white cannot hold with precise play. } Kh7 {[%clk 0:01:33.5] this endgame was actually holdable but I had to prevent the a pawn from clamping down on my queenside. Here there is only 1 good move to hold the game} 42. Ke2 {[%clk 0:03:51.7] this was played relatively quickly,, as it's 15 + 10 I should give myself more time in the endgame/late middlegame, I'm too used to speeding up at the endgame in rapid when I had plenty of time to calculate. Part of this was also me tryinig to play the clock and my opponent's lower time. In the future I should never try to play the clock like this in 15+10 because it simply doesn't work out as well.  More important is to find the best moves}  (42. a4 {the point behind a4 is to fix black's pawn on a dark square and mine on a light square. If I allow him to play a4, then his pawn is now on a light ssquare and untouchable. }  ) Kg6 {[%clk 0:01:40.8] }  (42... Bf6  43. Bg3 Kg6) 43. Kf3